WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:06.060
- When we asked who you
wanted to see on Sound Field,

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:08.923
this is what you said.

00:00:09.926 --> 00:00:12.426
(noise music)

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.290
In the world of avant-garde music,

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:19.940
historians mostly talk about
white man's contributions

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.020
to the genre.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.370
Oftentimes overlooking the
presence and musical offerings

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:28.480
of the LGBTQ, black and POC artists.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.800
We wanna explore noise music, its origins

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.070
and the underrepresented
artists who create noise

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:35.283
and experimental music.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.070
One of those artists is the rising

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.240
underground musician Dreamcrusher.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.980
- I should make this very clear.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.370
I mainly call myself a noise musician

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.680
because white people hate when I do that.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.530
My music's a bunch of different stuff.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:50.923
You could like classify it as noise,

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:56.270
but I'm really not into the
whole genre purism thing.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:57.603
I think it's really whack.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.720
- My usual co-host Nahre Sol
is off working on her album.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.870
So I asked musician and
vocalist Linda Diaz to meet up

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.620
with Dreamcrusher in New York city.

00:01:08.253 --> 00:01:10.753
(noise music)

00:01:21.093 --> 00:01:21.926
- Who comes to your shows?

00:01:21.926 --> 00:01:25.380
And if it is a lot of
like black queer people

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.451
and or artists, how does
that influence them?

00:01:28.451 --> 00:01:29.379
Or how do you feel

00:01:29.379 --> 00:01:30.770
like that impacts them at your shows?

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:35.570
- Historically with punk shows
or noise shows or whatever.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.979
So many queer people

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:41.833
and just young people in POC,
like don't feel comfortable.

00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:44.333
(noise music)

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.750
Like a lot of queer kids
come to my shows now.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:59.470
It is nice to like say that you're a thing

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:02.470
and people who are also
that thing feel affirmed

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.470
by it and show up and show their face

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:08.510
to you and tell you how
your work affected them.

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:10.230
That's like, it's amazing.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:13.803
And it gives more meaning
to the work, which is cool.

00:02:14.685 --> 00:02:17.185
(noise music)

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:24.370
- So what is noise music?

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:26.360
Noise is hard to define
and most people agree

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:27.640
that it's subjective.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.683
As music theorist Torben Sangild put it,

00:02:30.613 --> 00:02:32.670
"What is noise to one person
can be meaningful to another;

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:33.910
what was considered an unpleasant

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.840
sound yesterday is not today."

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.450
The term noise dates back
to 1913 when futurist artist

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.620
and musician Luigi Russolo published

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.110
his groundbreaking manifesto,

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.227
'The Art of Noises'.

00:02:45.194 --> 00:02:47.120
Russolo said that the
industrial revolution created

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.770
an entirely new landscape
of sounds to explore.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.890
The noises of metal screeching
and the construction

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:53.530
of skyscrapers gave listeners a

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.970
greater capacity to experience new sounds.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.240
Another influence on
composes of the era was

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.110
World Wars one and two.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.770
Life was a lot bleaker back then

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.200
which led a lot of musicians
and composers to experiment

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.050
and move away from melodic music.

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:07.780
Since then, the genre has been shaped

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.580
by art movements such
as Dada and Surrealism.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:11.820
One of the greatest influences

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.810
in noise has been the Fluxus movement.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.030
These artists emphasize
the artistic process

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:17.560
over the finished product.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.760
Listen to Ben Patterson's paper piece

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.690
which was created by giving
five performers pieces of paper

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:25.663
and directions on how to
crumble, scrub, and twist them.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:35.810
Ben Patterson, a black
artist born in 1934 was one

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:37.770
of the co-founders of the Fluxus movement.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.130
But his contributions are
often overlooked for his peers

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.170
like Yoko Ono and John Cage.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.589
This gets us back to
Dreamcrusher, performing

00:03:44.589 --> 00:03:47.155
at Saint Vitus bar in Brooklyn, New York.

00:03:47.155 --> 00:03:49.655
(noise music)

00:03:59.856 --> 00:04:01.202
What does your music sound like

00:04:01.202 --> 00:04:02.773
or how do you make a song?

00:04:02.773 --> 00:04:05.841
- There was this kid that
like, had never gone to a show

00:04:05.841 --> 00:04:07.913
of mine before that like walked up to me.

00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:09.413
It was probably in Philly.

00:04:09.413 --> 00:04:13.082
I feel like kids are a
lot more vocal in Philly.

00:04:13.082 --> 00:04:14.814
Then he walks up to me and he goes,

00:04:14.814 --> 00:04:17.143
"That sounded like a plane crashing

00:04:17.143 --> 00:04:19.166
for like half an hour."

00:04:19.166 --> 00:04:21.666
(noise music)

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:32.423
I make all my music mainly on a computer.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.160
More recently I've been
using a lot more samples.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.940
Yeah I make like 90%
of my own sound effects

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:40.773
and stuff like that.

00:04:40.773 --> 00:04:42.073
I just downloaded a program.

00:04:43.766 --> 00:04:45.916
Honestly I still don't
know what I'm doing.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.074
I just like mess with stuff.

00:04:49.074 --> 00:04:50.800
And then like, hopefully
it comes out right.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:52.590
- What program did you start on?

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.080
Like, was it like a garage band situation?

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.840
Was it like a phone,
voice note type thing?

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.173
I still do most of my vocals on my phone.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:06.240
I still use FL Studio for
literally the first 12 years

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:07.890
that I made anything.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.550
It was the trial version

00:05:10.687 --> 00:05:13.550
and I couldn't save anything.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:15.100
And then if I like,
didn't like how it came

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:18.501
out I would like just do it again.

00:05:18.501 --> 00:05:21.001
(noise music)

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:27.000
Dreamcrusher's ability to record,

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.410
distribute, and design
their own handmade tapes

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:31.920
and albums, perform hundreds of shows

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.250
and create heavily nuanced
soundscapes with socially

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:37.080
and politically charged
lyrics has made them a staple

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.353
in the world of postmodern noise music.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:43.930
- I mean, put it like this

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:47.190
like, one of the times we played together

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.650
and Allusive saw you, was
just like, Oh, it sounds

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.670
like a planet is being born.

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:55.250
And I was like, yeah, yes.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:57.222
- That's what he said?

00:05:57.222 --> 00:05:58.603
- Yeah.

00:05:58.603 --> 00:05:59.870
But essentially, yeah,
you were a world builder

00:06:00.883 --> 00:06:03.940
and also just like a
sweet, like projecting

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:05.850
of love type of person.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:06.905
You know what I mean?

00:06:06.905 --> 00:06:07.880
Like there was a genuine sort of sense

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:09.593
in your spirit and what not, so.

00:06:11.062 --> 00:06:13.562
(noise music)

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:18.373
- Like when you first moved

00:06:18.373 --> 00:06:20.390
to New York, how many
shows were you playing?

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:22.260
- Oh yeah, oh yeah.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.720
I've played like, I
think 108 shows in 2015

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:26.920
when I first moved here.

00:06:28.081 --> 00:06:29.068
- [Linda] What!

00:06:29.068 --> 00:06:32.180
- I didn't have a place to
live and whoever I was playing

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:37.180
with, I would just like,
hey, anybody got a couch?

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:41.345
And I was like sleeping at
Penn station for a little bit.

00:06:41.345 --> 00:06:42.575
It was like, that was a mess.

00:06:42.575 --> 00:06:44.100
But like I made it to shows on time,

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:47.000
did sound check and everything like, ay.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.740
- [Linda] Do a lot of people think

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:51.973
of you as like the first
to do what you're doing?

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.290
- I think a lot of people think

00:06:56.293 --> 00:06:59.081
that, or it's the first
time they've seen somebody

00:06:59.081 --> 00:07:00.434
like me make it.

00:07:00.434 --> 00:07:01.284
And that's a lie.

00:07:02.985 --> 00:07:05.485
(noise music)

00:07:10.535 --> 00:07:12.350
Although Dreamcrusher
exerts their strong beliefs

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.320
on individualism in their music and art.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.030
They also consider themselves to be a part

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:20.240
of a community of black, POC
and queer noise musicians.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:21.307
They told the fader,

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:23.420
"I always ask people
to research the history

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.960
of people of color in noise
and queer people in noise.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:27.390
People aren't paying attention to that.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:30.150
So my main mission is that I
want people to stop treating me

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:31.360
like I'm an anomaly.

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:32.520
Because I'm not."

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.030
One of Dreamcrushers musical influences

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:37.750
is the renowned Japanese
noise artist Merzbow.

00:07:43.998 --> 00:07:45.700
Since the 70s and 1980s, Japan has been a

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:47.360
leading creator of noise music.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:49.390
So much so that the
music scene inspired its

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:51.580
own sub-genre called Japanoise.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.793
Music Journalist Cleary Mallard writes,

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.580
"Noise takes it further
than any other genre.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:00.360
Rhythm, melody, lyrics,
standard instruments:

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.520
all forgotten... Across the world

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.040
people experiment with this
style, but Japanoise was one

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.970
of the greatest catalysts
for its existence today."

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:10.290
Fans like Hijokaiden
and Melt-Banana thrashed

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:12.430
into the North American
music lexicon creating

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:15.610
a globalized alternative,
and more importantly, a level

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.870
of equality with white
noise bands like Sonic Youth

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:19.480
and Psychic TV.

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:20.680
Dreamcrusher was even featured

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:24.050
on a compilation curated by
Adult Swim, entitled 'noise'

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:26.910
alongside the music of
Merzbow and Melt-Banana.

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:29.380
But wait, the connection runs even deeper.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:31.960
Masami Akita of Merzbow and Dreamcrusher

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.810
are both influenced by
free-jazz and the other worldly

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:36.443
music experimentalists Sun Ra.

00:08:40.663 --> 00:08:43.080
(jazz music)

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:47.960
Free-Jazz is a form of
tonal experimentation

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:51.290
which approaches jazz improvisation
in unconventional ways

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:53.540
such as deconstructing chords and tempos

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:56.190
to create discordant
compositions in the 1950s

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:57.460
and 1960s.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:59.440
Artists like Albert Ayler, Cecil Taylor,

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:02.260
and poet and musician,
Amiri Baraka, all rejected

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:05.130
traditional jazz composition
to map new territory

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.690
and open doors for experimental
black artists to rebel

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:10.880
against resonant, syncopated,
politically neutral music.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.630
Instead, these artists spoke their minds

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.413
about the times they were living in.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:17.620
The rise of modern recording
and sound processing

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:20.870
techniques, specifically
distortion also influenced

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:23.552
the development of
electronic and noise music.

00:09:23.552 --> 00:09:26.710
An example of this is the
practice Musique Concrete.

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:28.810
Where the composer edits
and distorts recorded

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.710
sounds to make a piece of music.

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:32.440
Listen to 'Wings of The Delirious Demon'

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:34.290
by Ilhan Mimaroglu, composed

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:36.773
by manipulating the pitch
of a clarinet recording.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:45.200
The invention of the Moog synthesizer

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:48.800
in 1964 was a breakthrough
for experimental musicians.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:50.890
These instruments are
the precursors to a lot

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:53.070
of computer generated
sounds heard in experimental

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:54.500
and noise music.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.250
The instrument was brought
into the mainstream

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:59.240
by transgender artist,
Wendy Carlos and her album

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.890
'Switched-On Bach' in 1968.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:04.120
She later went on to produce
synthesize soundtracks

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:07.150
for Stanley Kubrick's 'Clockwork
Orange' and 'The Shining'.

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:09.640
So how were you inspired by Wendy Carlos?

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:14.640
- My mom's a big cinephile and
one of the first like movies

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:18.060
from like her era was 'Clockwork Orange'.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:21.290
It's so sick to see like something

00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:25.420
like that, because, so I don't know.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:28.670
I just, almost every other
image of music I ever saw was

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:30.660
like people playing real instruments.

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:33.250
- New York's downtown music
scene was also an influence

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.320
on the formation of noise music.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:37.820
Set off by Yoko Ono in
1960 when she opened

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:41.050
her downtown loft for
unconventional music performances.

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:42.510
The live music downtown differed

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:44.990
from the more traditional
classical music played uptown

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:46.420
by the Lincoln Center.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:48.540
One of the musicians of
New York's downtown scene

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.040
what's minimalist
composer, Julius Eastman.

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:53.120
A lot of his avant-garde
compositions focused

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:54.970
on his identity as a black queer man.

00:11:06.107 --> 00:11:07.060
Why do your fans love you so much?

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:08.460
Why do they love your music?

00:11:10.645 --> 00:11:13.196
(noise music)

00:11:13.196 --> 00:11:16.823
- I hope it's because they
escaped through my work.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:22.680
I hope that it's because I provide

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:25.763
something very unique musically for them.

00:11:27.052 --> 00:11:29.552
(noise music)

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:35.500
I don't see a lot of black
non binary people like being

00:11:36.639 --> 00:11:41.639
in Pitchfork and Stereogum
like on NPR or PBS.

00:11:43.946 --> 00:11:46.630
(laughing)

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:49.050
But like, I hope that like, people seeing

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:52.880
me will like open the, not open
the flood gates necessarily.

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:55.692
Cause hey, they ain't that
many of us, but there's enough

00:11:55.692 --> 00:11:57.609
of us to like, come on.

00:11:58.733 --> 00:12:00.620
- Do you feel like it is a political act,

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:01.540
the art that you make,

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:02.990
or you as an artist?

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:05.130
- I'm just making things
that make me happy.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:09.030
If that's what it takes to
affirm other black queer artists

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:11.610
or people of color who want to make

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:13.940
something that they didn't grow up around

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.356
or like get any affirmation from it.

00:12:16.356 --> 00:12:17.610
And it's like, all right, like I want

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:20.363
to like be able to make
space for them too.

00:12:21.537 --> 00:12:24.037
(noise music)

